            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\r\n\r\nThis seems to be a result of `<C-o>` and `lua` command: it restores cursor to its original location. Similar to typing in Insert mode `<Esc>gcc` followed by `gi`.\r\n\r\nThe reason this is happening is because Neovim marks (used here) are fixed `(line, column)` pairs. Mechanism which respects adding and removing text is called extmarks. I tried to come up with solution for your case (set extmark, toggle line, set cursor to extmark location). But it also doesn't work since 'mini.comment' changes whole line for efficiency and extmark is moved to the next line.\r\n\r\nThe 'mini.comment' doesn't support Insert mode (and, probably, won't) and I can't see a way to fix this on 'mini.comment' side.\r\nSo sorry, I'll close this.\r\n\r\nFor reference, here is the code I tried for incorporating extmarks:\r\n```lua\r\nlocal ns_id = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('MyInsCommentLine')\r\n_G.comment_line_insert_mode = function()\r\n  local cur_pos = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\r\n  local extmark_id = vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns_id, cur_pos[1] - 1, cur_pos[2], {})\r\n\r\n  vim.cmd(string.format('lockmarks lua MiniComment.toggle_lines(%d, %d)', cur_pos[1], cur_pos[1]))\r\n  local new_pos = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_extmark_by_id(0, ns_id, extmark_id, {})\r\n  vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, { new_pos[1] + 1, new_pos[2] })\r\n\r\n  vim.api.nvim_buf_del_extmark(0, ns_id, extmark_id)\r\nend\r\n\r\nvim.keymap.set('i', '<M-m>', '<Cmd>lua _G.comment_line_insert_mode()<CR>')\r\n```",
